INT. ABANDONED BOWLING ALLEY - BASEMENT VAULT - NIGHT

Dust and rust. A hand-cranked lamp throws a pool of yellow on a steel door. Behind the pins, behind the lanes, behind forty years of silence: the vault.

Four crew members cluster around it.

OSWALDO "OZZIE" QUINTERO (50s, safecracker, stethoscope around his neck) presses an ear to the dial. LORELEI FAIRBANKS (30s, black turtleneck, a laptop on a milk crate) types in the shadows. GRANT BELLAMY (40s, plumber's overalls, a pry bar) leans against the wall. And in the doorway, watching the stairs, stands MAE-LING TSANG (20s, hood up, earpiece).

OZZIE
Quiet, please.

LORELEI
We are quiet.

GRANT
I'm quieter.

MAE-LING
(whispering)
Both of you are loud.

Ozzie turns the dial a quarter inch. Clicks. Turns it back. Clicks. His lips move. He is counting.

OZZIE
Seven. Nineteen. Four.

LORELEI
Your numbers or the bank's?

OZZIE
The bank's. Nineteen-eighty-six. The manager's anniversary.

GRANT
That's romantic.

OZZIE
It's lazy.

Lorelei looks up from the laptop. Grant straightens. Mae-Ling stiffens, one hand rising to her earpiece. She turns.

MAE-LING
Someone's on the stairs.

Grant lifts the pry bar. Lorelei closes the laptop. Ozzie keeps his hand on the dial, ear still pressed to the steel. Mae-Ling slips into the shadows beside the stairs, back against the wall.

Footsteps descend. Slow. Heavy. A flashlight beam sweeps the room.

A security guard, OFFICER DELBERT MARCH (60s, belly, jangling keys), steps off the last stair. He looks around. Sees nothing but dust, shadows, the glint of an old lane.

DELBERT
Hello?

Silence. He steps forward. His beam crosses the vault door, then the empty milk crate, then a coil of cable.

DELBERT (CONT'D)
Kids again.

He turns. Heads back up. Mae-Ling exhales. Ozzie clicks the dial one last time. The vault door shudders, groans, and swings open an inch.

Inside, in the glow of the lamp: a single gold bowling ball on a velvet cushion.

LORELEI
That's it?

GRANT
That's it.

OZZIE
It's solid gold.

MAE-LING
It's heavy.

GRANT
Of course it's heavy. It's gold.

Ozzie reaches in and lifts it with both hands. The floor creaks beneath him. Above, a door slams, and the flashlight beam returns, swinging fast down the stairs.

DELBERT (O.S.)
I knew it! Don't move!

All four crew members freeze. Ozzie hugs the ball to his chest. Grant raises the pry bar. Lorelei grabs the laptop. Mae-Ling reaches for the nearest bowling pin.

DELBERT (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Drop it!

Ozzie looks at the others. They look at him. He takes a step backward, rolls the golden ball down the nearest lane, and watches it thunder into the dark.

The pins explode. Delbert shouts. In the chaos, the crew scatters.
